WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.280
When you're trying to learn, your tendency can be to re-read what you're learning, or

2
00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:09.960
highlight or underline.

3
00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:16.920
But the best way to learn involves what researchers call retrieval practice.

4
00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:22.319
Here's what's happening in your brain when you learn something, anything.

5
00:00:22.319 --> 00:00:28.479
You're creating links between neurons in long-term memory.

6
00:00:28.479 --> 00:00:36.599
Each time you retrieve those ideas from your own mind, you're strengthening those sets

7
00:00:36.599 --> 00:00:37.959
of links.

8
00:00:37.959 --> 00:00:45.200
Hundreds of studies have shown that retrieval practice—that is, retrieving the ideas from

9
00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:50.680
your own mind—is the best approach to learning.

10
00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:57.520
Retrieval practice not only builds memory, but also significantly boosts your conceptual

11
00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:00.080
understanding.

12
00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:04.519
The most effective form of retrieval practice?

13
00:01:04.519 --> 00:01:07.519
Flashcards with spaced repetition.

14
00:01:07.519 --> 00:01:17.720
You can use AI tools like GPT, NotebookLM, and CLOD to generate personalized flashcards.

15
00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:27.080
More than ever, the flashcard landscape is rich with newer options besides those perennial

16
00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:31.319
favorites like Quizlet or Anki.

17
00:01:31.319 --> 00:01:33.440
But here's the key.

18
00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:42.360
Whatever app you choose, make sure it uses a spaced repetition algorithm for scheduling.

19
00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:46.199
This isn't just a nice-to-have feature.

20
00:01:46.199 --> 00:01:53.639
It's based on cognitive science and plenty of empirical evidence.

21
00:01:53.639 --> 00:01:59.800
Spaced repetition algorithms schedule your flashcard practice at the best interval to

22
00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:04.800
help combat your natural tendency to forget.

23
00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:12.720
When you rate how easily you recalled an answer, you're giving the algorithm crucial data

24
00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:16.479
about that memory's strength.

25
00:02:16.479 --> 00:02:23.539
The system then gives you that card again just before it predicts you'll forget.

26
00:02:23.539 --> 00:02:30.360
With each retrieval, you strengthen your memory for that concept.

27
00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:36.759
A spaced repetition algorithm is helpful in several important ways.

28
00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:46.320
First, the algorithm handles all the scheduling, so cards automatically appear when needed.

29
00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:49.479
No mental overhead for you.

30
00:02:49.479 --> 00:02:56.960
Second, you'll practice fewer cards daily because the system is intelligently spacing

31
00:02:56.960 --> 00:02:58.919
them out.

32
00:02:58.919 --> 00:03:07.199
Most importantly, this timing creates what psychologists call a desirable difficulty.

33
00:03:07.199 --> 00:03:16.119
That sweet spot where struggling to recall actually strengthens your long-term memory.

34
00:03:16.119 --> 00:03:21.360
Here's a challenge you probably experienced with traditional flashcards.

35
00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:28.479
Sometimes when you're struggling to remember something, just seeing the right answer isn't

36
00:03:28.479 --> 00:03:30.119
enough.

37
00:03:30.119 --> 00:03:34.399
Maybe you're studying and you see a question you know you should remember, but you're

38
00:03:34.399 --> 00:03:36.919
drawing a blank.

39
00:03:36.919 --> 00:03:43.360
The other side of the flashcard shows you the correct answer, but that isolated fact

40
00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:49.240
doesn't help you understand why it's the answer or how it connects to everything else

41
00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:51.600
you've been learning.

42
00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:59.479
I've been experimenting with a platform called smarterhumans.ai.

43
00:03:59.479 --> 00:04:03.759
It tackles this problem in an interesting way.

44
00:04:03.759 --> 00:04:11.720
Instead of just showing you isolated answers, every flashcard is deep-linked directly to

45
00:04:11.720 --> 00:04:15.619
the exact location where you learned that content.

46
00:04:15.619 --> 00:04:24.019
So instead of just staring at an answer and feeling confused, you can click a button and

47
00:04:24.019 --> 00:04:30.540
instantly jump back to the exact section of your textbook, video, or notes where that

48
00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:36.579
concept was originally explained in full context.

49
00:04:36.579 --> 00:04:42.899
SmarterHumans.ai also has a browser extension for creating flashcards from web pages or

50
00:04:42.899 --> 00:04:44.980
YouTube videos.

51
00:04:44.980 --> 00:04:50.420
And I can import my Kindle highlights to make flashcards.

52
00:04:50.420 --> 00:04:56.660
I can also create AI-generated summary notes, and there's a note-taking section where I

53
00:04:56.660 --> 00:05:02.660
can create flashcards deep-linked to my own notes.

54
00:05:02.660 --> 00:05:05.859
Let me show you how this works in practice.

55
00:05:05.859 --> 00:05:12.540
You can see how I have the browser extension pinned to the browser bar.

56
00:05:12.540 --> 00:05:19.820
I'll click it to open it up and generate a set of flashcards from one of my Coursera videos.

57
00:05:19.820 --> 00:05:27.019
Later, when you are practicing memory retrieval with one of these flashcards, if you struggle

58
00:05:27.019 --> 00:05:33.179
to recall the answer, you can click the source button and the source video will open at the

59
00:05:33.179 --> 00:05:39.040
relevant time where the content of the flashcard is discussed.

60
00:05:39.040 --> 00:05:46.399
The beauty of this approach is that you're not just memorizing isolated facts.

61
00:05:46.399 --> 00:05:51.880
You're building rich, interconnected knowledge networks.

62
00:05:51.880 --> 00:05:58.600
Each time you return to the original context, you're strengthening not just that specific

63
00:05:58.600 --> 00:06:03.519
memory but all the related concepts around it.

64
00:06:03.519 --> 00:06:12.480
The key takeaway here isn't necessarily any particular platform, but the principle.

65
00:06:12.480 --> 00:06:18.480
Retrieval practice with spaced repetition is your most powerful learning tool.

66
00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:25.500
If you can link that practice back to rich context, you're building the kind of deep

67
00:06:25.500 --> 00:06:29.000
understanding that actually sticks.